[Dong Zhuo](char-dong-zhuo) wrested the sword from her in alarm and took her in his arms. "I jest!" [Diaochan](char-diaochan) sank against his breast, hid her face and wept: "This must be [Li Ru](char-li-ru)'s doing! He and [Lü Bu](char-lu-bu) are close friends, and so he has laid this plan, caring nothing for your honor nor for my life. I could eat his flesh alive!" Dong Zhuo said: "How could I bear to part with you?" Diaochan said: "Though you love me as you do, I dare not stay here long, for Lü Bu will surely work me harm." Dong Zhuo said: "Tomorrow you and I go back to [Meiwu](loc-meiwu) together, and we shall be merry there. Fear no more." Then Diaochan dried her tears and bowed her thanks. Next day Li Ru came in and said: "Today is a lucky day. Diaochan may be sent to Lü Bu." Dong Zhuo said: "Lü Bu and I stand as father and son, and it would not be fitting to give her to him. I will do no more than pass over his fault. Tell him my mind, and comfort him with good words." Li Ru said: "You must not let a woman befool you, Grand Preceptor." Dong Zhuo's face changed. "Would you give your own wife to Lü Bu? Not another word of Diaochan. Speak of it again and I will have your head!" Li Ru went out, and looking up to heaven he sighed: "We shall all of us die by a woman's hand!" Later men, reading thus far, made a poem in lament:
> A red skirt bore [the Minister](char-wang-yun)'s design;
> No spear was drawn, no soldier stood in line.
> Three fights at [Hulao](loc-hulao-pass) spent their strength in vain;
> At Fengyi Pavilion rose the victor's strain.

[Wang Yun](char-wang-yun) would not hear [Ma Midi](char-ma-midi), but had [Cai Yong](char-cai-yong) put in prison and strangled there, and the scholars and officers of the court all wept for him. Later men argued that Cai Yong did wrong to weep for [Dong Zhuo](char-dong-zhuo), but also that Wang Yun went too far in killing him. There is a poem in lament:
> Dong Zhuo held all and gave his malice rein;
> Why did Cai Yong himself his death ordain?
> In those days [Zhuge Liang](char-zhuge-liang) at Longzhong lay;
> Would he have stooped a traitor to obey?
